(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_electrostaticpotentialandcapacitance=[{id:"neet-2026r-q013",pn:13,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential energy of point charge",q:`Consider a fixed uniformly charged insulating sphere with radius R and total charge +Q. A point charge \u2013q (q<<Q) with mass m is released from rest at a distance of 3R from the centre of the charged sphere. When the point charge reaches the surface of the sphere, its speed is:
(\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of vacuum, neglect gravitational forces).`,o:["\u221A(2Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(Qq/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(3Qq/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))"],c:1,s:`The charge starts at A (distance 3R, speed 0) and reaches the surface B (distance R).
Gain in kinetic energy = q \xD7 (potential difference), as the charge is attracted.
(1/2)mv\xB2 = q[KQ/R \u2212 KQ/(3R)].
(1/2)mv\xB2 = (KQq/R)(2/3).
v\xB2 = (KQq \xD7 4)/(3Rm) = (1 \xD7 Qq \xD7 4)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 3Rm) = Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080Rm).
v = \u221A(Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR)).`,fig:{w:350,h:125,alt:"Fixed sphere of radius R with charge +Q; point B on its surface and point A holding charge -q at distance 2R from B on a dotted line, with u = 0 at A.",d:[["c",113,55,48],["p","M65 55 Q113 42 161 55 Q113 68 65 55 Z","f"],["c",113,55,2,"F"],["l",113,55,138,18,"a"],["t",104,36,"R"],["t",75,12,"+Q"],["l",162,55,270,55,"d"],["c",162,55,2,"F"],["c",270,55,2.5,"F"],["t",166,67,"B","s"],["t",274,40,"-q"],["t",274,52,"A"],["l",166,78,268,78,"b"],["t",217,74,"2R","m"],["t",274,82,"(u = 0)"],["t",113,120,"(fixed)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Series and parallel capacitors",q:`Five capacitors of capacitances C\u2081 = C\u2082 = C\u2083 = C\u2084 = 10 \u03BCF and C\u2085 = 2.5 \u03BCF are connected as shown, along with a battery of 50 V .
The equivalent capacitance and the charges on each capacitor respectively are:`,o:["5 \u03BCF, 125 \u03BCC on all capacitors","5 \u03BCF, 250 \u03BCC on all capacitors","4 \u03BCF, 250 \u03BCC on C\u2081 to C\u2084 and 125 \u03BCC on C\u2085","5 \u03BCF, 125 \u03BCC on C\u2081 to C\u2084 and 25 \u03BCC on C\u2085"],c:0,s:`The four capacitors C\u2081 to C\u2084 are in series: their equivalent is 2.5 \u03BCF (10/4).
This 2.5 \u03BCF is in parallel with C\u2085 = 2.5 \u03BCF.
C_eq = 2.5 + 2.5 = 5 \u03BCF.
Charge on each series capacitor: q\u2081 = q\u2082 = q\u2083 = q\u2084 = 2.5 \xD7 50 = 125 \u03BCC.
Charge on C\u2085: q\u2085 = 2.5 \xD7 50 = 125 \u03BCC.`,fig:{w:185,h:215,alt:"Series chain C1 to C4 around the loop in parallel with C5, across a 50 V battery",d:[["l",20,22,47,22],["l",47,14,47,30],["l",57,14,57,30],["l",57,22,107,22],["l",107,14,107,30],["l",117,14,117,30],["l",117,22,148,22],["t",52,10,"C\u2082","m"],["t",112,10,"C\u2083","m"],["l",20,22,20,84],["l",13,86,27,86],["l",13,92,27,92],["l",20,95,20,178],["l",148,22,148,84],["l",141,86,155,86],["l",141,92,155,92],["l",148,95,148,178],["t",29,92,"C\u2081"],["t",157,92,"C\u2084"],["l",20,148,81,148],["l",79,140,79,156],["l",89,140,89,156],["l",89,148,148,148],["t",84,132,"C\u2085","m"],["l",20,178,82,178],["l",90,178,148,178],["l",83,169,83,187],["l",89,173,89,183],["t",84,202,"50 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:315,h:160,alt:"Left: four capacitors of 1 in series in parallel with 2.5, across 50 V. Right: two 2.5 microfarad capacitors in parallel across 50 V",d:[["t",8,16,"C\u2081 = C\u2082 = C\u2083 = C\u2084 = 1","s"],["l",10,35,34,35],["l",34,27,34,43],["l",44,27,44,43],["l",44,35,66,35],["l",66,27,66,43],["l",76,27,76,43],["l",76,35,108,35],["l",108,27,108,43],["l",118,27,118,43],["l",118,35,146,35],["l",146,27,146,43],["l",156,27,156,43],["l",156,35,190,35],["l",10,35,10,125],["l",190,35,190,125],["l",10,80,76,80],["l",75,72,75,88],["l",85,72,85,88],["l",85,80,190,80],["t",82,70,"C\u2085 = 2.5","s"],["l",10,125,92,125],["l",98,125,190,125],["l",92,116,92,134],["l",98,120,98,130],["t",100,146,"50 V","m"],["l",225,15,225,125],["l",300,15,300,125],["l",225,25,257,25],["l",257,17,257,33],["l",267,17,267,33],["l",267,25,300,25],["l",225,70,257,70],["l",257,62,257,78],["l",267,62,267,78],["l",267,70,300,70],["t",262,12,"2.5 \u03BCF","m"],["t",262,57,"2.5 \u03BCF","m"],["l",225,125,258,125],["l",264,125,300,125],["l",258,116,258,134],["l",264,120,264,130],["t",262,146,"50 V","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q016",pn:16,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Work done in electric field",q:`A unit positive point charge is taken slowly through an infinitesimally thin tube that is inside a charged dielectric sphere of radius R, having uniform positive charge density \u03C1, as shown in the figure. The initial and final positions of the charge are marked by A and B at distance 2R and 3R respectively, from the centre of the sphere. In this process, the magnitude of the total work done on the point charge is \u03C1R\xB2/(n\u03B5\u2080). The value of n is :
(\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of vacuum)`,o:["6","9","18","2"],c:2,s:`Work done W = q(V_B \u2212 V_A).
W = q[KQ/(3R) \u2212 KQ/(2R)] = (Kq/R)(\u2212Q/6).
|W| = KQq/(6R) = (1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)) \xD7 (q/R) \xD7 (1/6) \xD7 \u03C1 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3.
|W| = \u03C1R\xB2/(18\u03B5\u2080), as q = 1.
So n = 18.`,fig:{w:280,h:125,alt:"Charged sphere of radius R with density rho and centre O; A at distance 2R and B at distance 3R from O; a dashed arrow from A to B passes through the sphere as a thin tube.",d:[["c",159,64,46],["l",159,64,242,34],["l",27,51,159,64],["l",242,34,27,51,"da"],["l",205,37,114,47,"dB"],["c",242,34,2.5,"F"],["c",27,51,2.5,"F"],["c",159,64,1.5,"F"],["t",250,34,"A"],["t",10,66,"B"],["t",76,73,"3R","m"],["t",196,68,"2R"],["t",164,78,"O"],["t",128,84,"\u03C1"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q021",pn:21,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Electrostatic shielding",q:"A point charge Q is placed inside a cavity within a solid isolated conducting sphere. Consider points A, B and C as shown in the figure, where the magnitudes of the electric fields are E_A, E_B, E_C, respectively. The points B and C are at the same distance from the center of the solid sphere. The correct option is :",o:["E_A \u2260 0, E_B = E_C","E_A = 0, E_B > E_C","E_A \u2260 0, E_B < E_C","E_A = 0, E_B = E_C"],c:0,s:`Due to the charge Q inside the cavity, E_A \u2260 0.
The charge spreads uniformly on the outer surface of the conducting sphere.
B and C are at the same distance from the centre of the sphere.
So E_B = E_C (electrostatic shielding).`,fig:{w:350,h:165,alt:"Solid conducting sphere with an off-centre cavity containing charge Q and point A; points C and B lie outside the sphere on the left and right along a dotted horizontal line.",d:[["l",15,82,338,82,"d"],["c",160,82,75,"f"],["c",193,82,25],["t",178,74,"Q","s"],["c",193,82,1.5,"F"],["t",183,100,"A","s"],["c",198,98,2,"F"],["c",41,82,2.5,"F"],["t",38,70,"C","s"],["c",275,82,2.5,"F"],["t",270,70,"B","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:195,alt:"Sphere with minus charges on the cavity wall around Q and plus charges spread uniformly on the outer surface; centre O, points C on the left and B on the right outside on a dotted line.",d:[["l",5,90,295,90,"d"],["c",130,100,75,"f"],["c",166,82,25],["t",162,83,"Q","s"],["t",183,85,"\u2212","s"],["t",145,85,"\u2212","s"],["t",151,72,"\u2212","s"],["t",164,66,"\u2212","s"],["t",177,72,"\u2212","s"],["t",211,103,"+","s"],["t",208,125,"+","s"],["t",200,145,"+","s"],["t",186,162,"+","s"],["t",169,176,"+","s"],["t",149,184,"+","s"],["t",127,187,"+","s"],["t",105,184,"+","s"],["t",85,176,"+","s"],["t",68,162,"+","s"],["t",54,145,"+","s"],["t",46,125,"+","s"],["t",43,103,"+","s"],["t",46,81,"+","s"],["t",54,61,"+","s"],["t",68,44,"+","s"],["t",85,30,"+","s"],["t",105,22,"+","s"],["t",127,19,"+","s"],["t",149,22,"+","s"],["t",169,30,"+","s"],["t",186,44,"+","s"],["t",200,61,"+","s"],["t",208,81,"+","s"],["c",128,90,1.5,"F"],["t",122,102,"O","s"],["t",160,108,"A","s"],["c",33,90,2.5,"F"],["t",29,82,"C","s"],["c",227,90,2.5,"F"],["t",229,82,"B","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q030",pn:30,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Energy stored in capacitors",q:"Three identical capacitors, P, Q and S, each of the capacitance C, are connected to a battery of voltage V, as shown in the figure. If the energy stored in the capacitor P and total energy stored in the system are U_P and U_T, respectively, then the ratio U_P/U_T is:",o:["1/3","1/2","1/6","2/3"],c:2,s:`C_P = C_Q = C_S = C.
P and Q are in series with each other, so each gets V/2.
This series pair is in parallel with S, so S gets the full V.
U_P = (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 = (1/2)(CV\xB2/4) = CV\xB2/8.
U_T = U_P + U_Q + U_S = (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 + (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 + (1/2)CV\xB2
U_T = CV\xB2/8 + CV\xB2/8 + (1/2)CV\xB2 = (6/8)CV\xB2 = (3/4)CV\xB2.
U_P/U_T = (CV\xB2/8)/(3CV\xB2/4) = 1/6.`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Capacitors P and Q in series on the top branch, capacitor S on a parallel branch below, and a battery V on the bottom wire across the combination.",d:[["l",60,30,97,30],["l",97,22,97,38],["l",103,22,103,38],["l",103,30,167,30],["l",167,22,167,38],["l",173,22,173,38],["l",173,30,200,30],["l",60,30,60,100],["l",200,30,200,100],["l",60,100,127,100],["l",127,92,127,108],["l",133,92,133,108],["l",133,100,200,100],["l",60,68,30,68],["l",30,68,30,150],["l",200,68,230,68],["l",230,68,230,150],["l",30,150,127,150],["l",127,141,127,159],["l",133,145,133,155,"B"],["l",133,150,230,150],["t",100,16,"P","m"],["t",170,16,"Q","m"],["t",140,118,"S"],["t",130,172,"V","m"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:160,alt:"Redrawn circuit: P and Q in series on the top branch, S on the middle branch, battery V on the bottom branch, joined by the left and right vertical wires.",d:[["l",30,30,97,30],["l",97,22,97,38],["l",103,22,103,38],["l",103,30,167,30],["l",167,22,167,38],["l",173,22,173,38],["l",173,30,230,30],["l",30,30,30,130],["l",230,30,230,130],["l",30,80,127,80],["l",127,72,127,88],["l",133,72,133,88],["l",133,80,230,80],["l",30,130,127,130],["l",127,121,127,139],["l",133,125,133,135,"B"],["l",133,130,230,130],["t",100,16,"P","m"],["t",170,16,"Q","m"],["t",140,98,"S"],["t",130,152,"V","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Sharing of charge between capacitors",q:"Consider two uncharged capacitors of equal capacitance 200 pF. One of them is charged by a 100 V supply and disconnected. Now this capacitor is connected to the uncharged capacitor. The amount of electrostatic energy lost in the process is:",o:["1.0\xD710\u207B\u2076 J","0.5\xD710\u207B\u2076 J","0.5 J","1.0 J"],c:1,s:`Energy loss = (1/2) \xD7 (C\u2081C\u2082)/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) \xD7 V\xB2.
= (1/2) \xD7 (200\xD7200)/400 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 (100)\xB2.
= (1/2) \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 (since 200\xD7200/400 = 100 and 100 \xD7 10\u2074 = 10\u2076).
= 0.5\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.`},{id:"neet-2025-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Capacitor with dielectric slabs",q:`The plates of a parallel plate capacitor are separated by d. Two slabs of different dielectric constant K\u2081 and K\u2082 with thickness (3/8)d and d/2, respectively are inserted in the capacitor. Due to this, the capacitance becomes two times larger than when there is nothing between the plates.
If K\u2081 = 1.25 K\u2082, the value of K\u2081 is:`,o:["2.66","2.33","1.60","1.33"],c:0,s:`Use C_eq = \u03B5\u2080A/(t\u2081/K\u2081 + t\u2082/K\u2082 + t\u2083/K\u2083).
Here C\u2080 = \u03B5\u2080A/d, t\u2081 = 3d/8, t\u2082 = d/2, t\u2083 = d/8 (the remaining air gap).
K\u2081 = K\u2081, K\u2082 = K\u2081/1.25 and K\u2083 = 1
Given C_eq = 2C\u2080
2\u03B5\u2080A/d = \u03B5\u2080A/(3d/(8K\u2081) + d/(2K\u2081) \xD7 5/4 + d/8)
2 = 1/(3/(8K\u2081) + 5/(8K\u2081) + 1/8)
K\u2081 = 8/3 = 2.66`,sfig:{w:220,h:270,alt:"Capacitor plates drawn vertically with total gap d and three layers side by side: t1 with K1 (slanted hatching), t2 with K2 (horizontal lines) and t3 with K3 (dotted, air).",d:[["l",13.6,21.3,13.6,193.8,""],["l",63.8,21.3,63.8,193.8,""],["l",119,23.8,119,193.8,""],["l",170.9,21.3,170.9,193.8,""],["l",13.6,21.3,63.8,21.3,""],["l",63.8,23.8,119,23.8,""],["l",119,23.8,170.9,23.8,""],["l",13.6,38.3,63.8,22.1,""],["l",13.6,54.4,63.8,38.3,""],["l",13.6,70.6,63.8,54.4,""],["l",13.6,86.7,63.8,70.6,""],["l",13.6,119,63.8,102.9,""],["l",13.6,135.2,63.8,119,""],["l",13.6,151.3,63.8,135.2,""],["l",13.6,167.5,63.8,151.3,""],["l",13.6,183.6,63.8,167.5,""],["l",63.8,30.6,119,30.6,""],["l",119,30.6,170.9,30.6,"d"],["l",63.8,37.4,119,37.4,""],["l",119,37.4,170.9,37.4,"d"],["l",63.8,44.2,119,44.2,""],["l",119,44.2,170.9,44.2,"d"],["l",63.8,51,119,51,""],["l",119,51,170.9,51,"d"],["l",63.8,57.8,119,57.8,""],["l",119,57.8,170.9,57.8,"d"],["l",63.8,64.6,119,64.6,""],["l",119,64.6,170.9,64.6,"d"],["l",63.8,71.4,119,71.4,""],["l",119,71.4,170.9,71.4,"d"],["l",63.8,78.2,119,78.2,""],["l",119,78.2,170.9,78.2,"d"],["l",63.8,85,119,85,""],["l",119,85,170.9,85,"d"],["l",63.8,91.8,119,91.8,""],["l",119,91.8,170.9,91.8,"d"],["l",63.8,119,119,119,""],["l",119,119,170.9,119,"d"],["l",63.8,125.8,119,125.8,""],["l",119,125.8,170.9,125.8,"d"],["l",63.8,132.6,119,132.6,""],["l",119,132.6,170.9,132.6,"d"],["l",63.8,139.4,119,139.4,""],["l",119,139.4,170.9,139.4,"d"],["l",63.8,146.2,119,146.2,""],["l",119,146.2,170.9,146.2,"d"],["l",63.8,153,119,153,""],["l",119,153,170.9,153,"d"],["l",63.8,159.8,119,159.8,""],["l",119,159.8,170.9,159.8,"d"],["l",63.8,166.6,119,166.6,""],["l",119,166.6,170.9,166.6,"d"],["l",63.8,173.4,119,173.4,""],["l",119,173.4,170.9,173.4,"d"],["l",63.8,180.2,119,180.2,""],["l",119,180.2,170.9,180.2,"d"],["l",63.8,187,119,187,""],["l",119,187,170.9,187,"d"],["l",63.8,193.8,119,193.8,""],["l",119,193.8,170.9,193.8,"d"],["t",34,17.8,"t\u2081","m"],["t",95.2,17.8,"t\u2082","m"],["t",149.6,17.8,"t\u2083","m"],["t",33.2,101,"K\u2081","m"],["t",96.9,109.7,"K\u2082","m"],["t",153,109.7,"K\u2083","m"],["l",82.5,210,10.2,210,"a"],["l",102.9,210,171.7,210,"a"],["l",8.5,204,8.5,215.9,""],["l",173.4,204,173.4,215.9,""],["t",92.6,214.2,"d","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Equivalent capacitance, balanced bridge",q:"In the following circuit, the equivalent capacitance between terminal A and terminal B is-",o:["0.5 \u03BCF","4 \u03BCF","2 \u03BCF","1 \u03BCF"],c:2,s:`The circuit is a balanced Wheatstone-type bridge, so the middle 2 \u03BCF capacitor carries no charge and can be removed.
The top branch is two 2 \u03BCF in series = 1 \u03BCF.
The bottom branch is two 2 \u03BCF in series = 1 \u03BCF.
The two branches are in parallel: C(AB) = 1 + 1 = 2 \u03BCF.`,fig:{w:265,h:145,alt:"Terminals A and B joined by two branches of two 2 \u03BCF capacitors in series, with a 2 \u03BCF capacitor bridging the two middle junctions.",d:[["c",14,70,3],["l",17,70,45,70],["l",45,25,45,115],["l",220,25,220,115],["l",220,70,247,70],["c",250,70,3],["l",45,25,92,25],["l",98,25,172,25],["l",178,25,220,25],["l",45,115,92,115],["l",98,115,172,115],["l",178,115,220,115],["l",92,16,92,34],["l",98,16,98,34],["l",92,106,92,124],["l",98,106,98,124],["t",95,12,"2\u03BCF","m"],["t",95,138,"2\u03BCF","m"],["l",172,16,172,34],["l",178,16,178,34],["l",172,106,172,124],["l",178,106,178,124],["t",175,12,"2\u03BCF","m"],["t",175,138,"2\u03BCF","m"],["l",135,25,135,63],["l",135,67,135,115],["l",127,63,143,63],["l",127,67,143,67],["t",148,70,"2\u03BCF"],["c",135,25,1.6,"F"],["c",135,115,1.6,"F"],["t",6,88,"A"],["t",246,88,"B"]]},sfig:{w:265,h:145,alt:"The same circuit without the middle capacitor: two branches, each two 2 \u03BCF capacitors in series, in parallel between A and B.",d:[["c",14,70,3],["l",17,70,45,70],["l",45,25,45,115],["l",220,25,220,115],["l",220,70,247,70],["c",250,70,3],["l",45,25,92,25],["l",98,25,172,25],["l",178,25,220,25],["l",45,115,92,115],["l",98,115,172,115],["l",178,115,220,115],["l",92,16,92,34],["l",98,16,98,34],["l",92,106,92,124],["l",98,106,98,124],["t",95,12,"2\u03BCF","m"],["t",95,138,"2\u03BCF","m"],["l",172,16,172,34],["l",178,16,178,34],["l",172,106,172,124],["l",178,106,178,124],["t",175,12,"2\u03BCF","m"],["t",175,138,"2\u03BCF","m"],["t",6,88,"A"],["t",246,88,"B"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Dipole axial potential",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : The potential (V) at any axial point, at 2 m distance (r) from the centre of the dipole of dipole moment vector P of magnitude, 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C m, is \xB19 \xD7 10\xB3 V. (Take 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\xB3 SI units)
Reason R : V = \xB12P/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080r\xB2), where r is the distance of any axial point, situated at 2 m from the centre of the dipole.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A"],c:0,s:`The potential at distance r from the centre of a dipole is V = KP cos \u03B8 / r\xB2.
On the axis at \u03B8 = 0\xB0: V = KP/r\xB2 = (9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/2\xB2 = 9 \xD7 10\xB3 V.
On the axis at \u03B8 = 180\xB0: V = \u2212KP/r\xB2 = \u22129 \xD7 10\xB3 V.
So A is true (\xB19 \xD7 10\xB3 V). The formula in R has a factor 2P, but the correct one is P, so R is false.
Note: The printed text gives 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\xB3, but the solution uses 9 \xD7 10\u2079 (the correct value).`},{id:"neet-2024-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential of charged shell",q:"A thin spherical shell is charged by some source. The potential difference between the two points C and P (in V) shown in the figure is (Take 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 SI units)",o:["0.5 \xD7 10\u2075","zero","3 \xD7 10\u2075","1 \xD7 10\u2075"],c:1,s:`For a uniformly charged spherical shell, V = kq/R for every point with r \u2264 R (inside and on the shell).
So V_C = V_P.
V_C \u2013 V_P = 0.`,fig:{w:220,h:200,alt:"A thin spherical shell of radius R = 3 cm carrying charge q = 1 \u03BCC, with its centre C and a point P on the shell joined to C by a dotted line.",d:[["c",110,95,62],["t",180,99,"+","m","s"],["t",178.5,113.6,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",173.9,127.5,"+","m","s"],["t",166.6,140.1,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",156.8,151,"+","m","s"],["t",145,159.6,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",131.6,165.6,"+","m","s"],["t",117.3,168.6,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",102.7,168.6,"+","m","s"],["t",88.4,165.6,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",75,159.6,"+","m","s"],["t",63.2,151,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",53.4,140.1,"+","m","s"],["t",46.1,127.5,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",41.5,113.6,"+","m","s"],["t",40,99,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",41.5,84.4,"+","m","s"],["t",46.1,70.5,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",53.4,57.9,"+","m","s"],["t",63.2,47,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",75,38.4,"+","m","s"],["t",88.4,32.4,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",102.7,29.4,"+","m","s"],["t",117.3,29.4,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",131.6,32.4,"+","m","s"],["t",145,38.4,"\xD7","m","s"],["t",173.9,70.5,"+","m","s"],["t",178.5,84.4,"\xD7","m","s"],["c",110,95,3,"F"],["l",110,95,66.2,138.8,"B"],["l",110,95,156.1,53.5,"d"],["t",100,87,"C","e"],["t",107,129,"R = 3 cm"],["t",176.1,47.5,"P"],["t",110,185,"q = 1 \u03BCC","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Parallel plate capacitor",q:`If the plates of a parallel plate capacitor connected to a battery are moved close to each other, then
A. the charge stored in it, increase.
B. the energy stored in it, decreases.
C. its capacitance increases.
D. the ratio of charge to its potential remains the same.
E. the product of charge and voltage increases.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["B, D and E only","A, B and C only","A, B and E only","A, C and E only"],c:3,s:`The battery stays connected, so V\u2032 = V (constant).
(i) C\u2032 = \u03B5\u2080A/d\u2032 and C = \u03B5\u2080A/d. Since d\u2032 < d, C\u2032 > C, so the capacitance increases.
(ii) U\u2032 = \xBDC\u2032V\xB2 and U = \xBDCV\xB2. Since C\u2032 > C, U\u2032 > U, so the energy increases.
(iii) Q\u2032/V\u2032 = C\u2032 and Q/V = C. Since C\u2032 \u2260 C, the ratio does not stay the same.
(iv) Product of charge and voltage: X\u2032 = Q\u2032V = C\u2032V\xB2 and X = QV = CV\xB2. So X\u2032 > X.
Also Q = CV increases because C increases.`},{id:"neet-2023-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Series and parallel capacitors",q:"The equivalent capacitance of the system shown in the following circuit is :",o:["9 \xB5F","2 \xB5F","3 \xB5F","6 \xB5F"],c:1,s:`The two 3 \xB5F capacitors in parallel give C\u2081 = 3 + 3 = 6 \xB5F.
This is in series with the other 3 \xB5F capacitor.
C_eq = (3\xD76)/(3+6) = 18/9 = 2 \xB5F.`,fig:{w:260,h:135,alt:"A is joined through a 3 \xB5F capacitor in series to two 3 \xB5F capacitors in parallel, which lead to B.",d:[["c",30,65,3,"F"],["l",30,65,66,65],["l",66,55,66,75],["l",73,55,73,75],["l",73,65,110,65],["l",110,30,110,100],["l",110,30,142,30],["l",142,20,142,40],["l",149,20,149,40],["l",149,30,190,30],["l",110,100,142,100],["l",142,90,142,110],["l",149,90,149,110],["l",149,100,190,100],["l",190,30,190,100],["l",190,65,225,65],["c",225,65,3,"F"],["t",19,69,"A","e"],["t",234,69,"B"],["t",70,47,"3\xB5F","m"],["t",146,14,"3\xB5F","m"],["t",146,126,"3\xB5F","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential due to a dipole",q:"An electric dipole is placed as shown in the figure. The electric potential (in 10\xB2 V) at point P due to the dipole is (\u03B5\u2080 = permittivity of free space and 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = K)",o:["(8/3) qK","(3/8) qK","(5/8) qK","(8/5) qK"],c:1,s:`Potential of a point charge is Kq/r, and potentials add.
P is 2 cm from +q and 8 cm from \u2013q.
V = Kq/(2\xD710\u207B\xB2) \u2212 Kq/(8\xD710\u207B\xB2) = (Kq\xD710\xB2/2)(1 \u2212 1/4).
V = (3/8) Kq \xD7 10\xB2 V, so the answer is (3/8) qK.`,fig:{w:310,h:140,alt:"Dipole on a line: \u2212q at the left, centre O, +q at the right, 3 cm either side of O; point P on the same line, 5 cm from O.",d:[["l",130,40,268,40,"a"],["t",200,32,"5 cm","m"],["t",130,76,"O","m"],["l",46,95,214,95],["c",46,95,3,"F"],["c",214,95,3,"F"],["c",270,95,3,"F"],["l",130,89,130,101],["t",276,50,"P"],["t",46,115,"\u2013q","m"],["t",214,115,"+q","m"],["l",128,122,48,122,"a"],["l",132,122,212,122,"a"],["t",88,136,"3 cm","m"],["t",172,136,"3 cm","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Equipotential surfaces",q:"The angle between the electric lines of force and the equipotential surface is",o:["45\xB0","90\xB0","180\xB0","0\xB0"],c:1,s:`dV = \u2013E \xB7 dr = \u2013E dr cos\u03B8.
On an equipotential surface dV = 0.
So cos\u03B8 = 0 and \u03B8 = 90\xB0.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q008",pn:8,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Parallel plate capacitor",q:"The distance between the two plates of a parallel plate capacitor is doubled and the area of each plate is halved. If C is its initial capacitance, its final capacitance is equal to:",o:["C/4","2C","C/2","4C"],c:0,s:`Initial capacitance C = (A\u03B5\u2080)/(d).
New capacitance C\u2032 = ((A/2)\u03B5\u2080)/(2d) = (A\u03B5\u2080)/(4d) = C/4.`},{id:"neet-2022-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential of a charged conducting sphere",q:"Two hollow conducting spheres of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082 (R\u2081 >> R\u2082) have equal charges. The potential would be",o:["More on smaller sphere","Equal on both the spheres","Dependent on the material property of the sphere","More on bigger sphere"],c:0,s:`Potential of a hollow conducting sphere is V = KQ/R.
The charge Q is the same on both.
So V \u221D 1/R: a larger radius gives a smaller potential.
Hence the potential is more on the smaller sphere.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q029",pn:29,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Series and parallel capacitors",q:"The effective capacitances of two capacitors are 3 \u03BCF and 16 \u03BCF, when they are connected in series and parallel respectively. The capacitance of two capacitors are:",o:["1.2 \u03BCF, 1.8 \u03BCF","10 \u03BCF, 6 \u03BCF","8 \u03BCF, 8 \u03BCF","12 \u03BCF, 4 \u03BCF"],c:3,s:`Let the capacitors be C\u2081 and C\u2082.
Series: C\u2081C\u2082/(C\u2081+C\u2082) = 3. Parallel: C\u2081 + C\u2082 = 16.
So C\u2081C\u2082 = 48.
(C\u2081 \u2212 C\u2082)\xB2 = (C\u2081+C\u2082)\xB2 \u2212 4C\u2081C\u2082 = 256 \u2212 192 = 64, so C\u2081 \u2212 C\u2082 = 8.
Solving, C\u2081 = 12 \u03BCF and C\u2082 = 4 \u03BCF.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q044",pn:44,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential at hexagon centre",q:"Six charges +q, \u2212q, \u2212q, +q, +q and \u2212q are fixed at the corners of a hexagon of side d as shown in the figure. The work done in bringing a charge q\u2080 to the centre of the hexagon from infinity is: (\u03B5\u2080 \u2013 permittivity of free space)",o:["(\u2212q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d))(6 \u2212 1/\u221A2)","Zero","\u2212q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d)","(\u2212q\xB2/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d))(3 \u2212 1/\u221A2)"],c:1,s:`All corners are at distance d from the centre.
Potential at the centre = (1/4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080d) \xD7 (sum of charges) = 0, since three charges are +q and three are \u2212q.
Work = q\u2080 \xD7 V = 0.
Note: The printed question text lists only five charges ("+q, \u2013q, \u2013q, +q and \u2013q"); the figure shows six, and the six are written here from the figure.`,fig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"Regular hexagon of side d with charges +q, -q, +q, -q, +q, -q... at its corners going round (top-left +q, top-right -q, right +q, bottom-right -q, bottom-left +q, left -q) and a charge q0 at the centre.",d:[["p","M109 34.3L171 34.3L202 88L171 141.7L109 141.7L78 88Z"],["c",140,88,2.6,"F"],["t",147,92,"q\u2080"],["t",109,28.306424965364805,"+q","m"],["t",171,28.306424965364805,"\u2212q","m"],["t",209,92,"+q"],["t",171,156.6935750346352,"\u2212q","m"],["t",109,156.6935750346352,"+q","m"],["t",71,92,"\u2212q","e"],["l",70.52564434701786,103.05403625519529,92.22564434701786,140.63953877943993,"b"],["t",66,122,"d","e"]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Sharing of charge between capacitors",q:"A capacitor of capacitance C = 900 pF is charged fully by 100 V battery B as shown in figure (a). Then it is disconnected from the battery and connected to another uncharged capacitor of capacitance C = 900 pF as shown in figure (b). The electrostatic energy stored by the system (b) is",o:["3.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","2.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J"],c:1,s:`Charge on the first capacitor q = CV = 900 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 100 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 C.
Common potential after joining V = (C\u2081V\u2081 + C\u2082V\u2082)/(C\u2081 + C\u2082) = (9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 + 0)/(1800 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2) = 50 V.
Energy U = \xBD(C\u2081 + C\u2082)V\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 1800 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 50 \xD7 50.
U = 2.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J.`,fig:{w:320,h:156,alt:"Figure (a): a capacitor C connected in a loop to a 100 V battery B. Figure (b): two capacitors C joined in a closed loop with no battery.",d:[["l",76,6,76,50],["l",85,6,85,50],["t",73,21,"+","e"],["t",88,21,"\u2212"],["t",73,32,"+","e"],["t",88,32,"\u2212"],["t",73,43,"+","e"],["t",88,43,"\u2212"],["p","M32 22.5H76"],["p","M85 22.5H136V120H84"],["p","M32 22.5V120H68"],["c",70,120,2],["c",82,120,2],["t",80,64,"C","m"],["t",76,112,"100 V","m"],["t",63,134,"+","m"],["t",90,134,"\u2212","m"],["t",76,148,"B","m"],["t",0,82,"(a)"],["l",234,6,234,50],["l",243,6,243,50],["t",231,21,"+","e"],["t",246,21,"+"],["t",231,32,"+","e"],["t",246,32,"+"],["t",231,43,"+","e"],["t",246,43,"+"],["l",234,70,234,114],["l",243,70,243,114],["t",231,85,"+","e"],["t",246,85,"\u2212"],["t",231,96,"+","e"],["t",246,96,"\u2212"],["t",231,107,"+","e"],["t",246,107,"\u2212"],["p","M190 22.5H234"],["p","M243 22.5H294V86.5H243"],["p","M190 22.5V86.5H234"],["t",238,64,"C","m"],["t",238,130,"C","m"],["t",164,76,"(b)"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:172,alt:"Charged capacitor C across 100 V, then the two capacitors C joined in parallel, each with the plus plate on the left and the minus plate on the right.",d:[["l",62,14,62,66],["l",71,14,71,66],["t",59,25,"+","e"],["t",74,25,"\u2212"],["t",59,44,"+","e"],["t",74,44,"\u2212"],["t",59,62,"+","e"],["t",74,62,"\u2212"],["p","M8 29.6H62"],["p","M71 29.6H126"],["l",8,29.6,8,112],["l",126,29.6,126,112],["l",44,118,8,118,"a"],["l",90,118,126,118,"a"],["t",67,122,"100 V","m"],["t",66,10,"C","m"],["l",214,14,214,66],["l",223,14,223,66],["t",211,25,"+","e"],["t",226,25,"\u2212"],["t",211,44,"+","e"],["t",226,44,"\u2212"],["t",211,62,"+","e"],["t",226,62,"\u2212"],["l",214,96,214,148],["l",223,96,223,148],["t",211,107,"+","e"],["t",226,107,"\u2212"],["t",211,126,"+","e"],["t",226,126,"\u2212"],["t",211,144,"+","e"],["t",226,144,"\u2212"],["p","M160 29.6H214"],["p","M223 29.6H278V111.6H223"],["p","M160 29.6V111.6H214"],["t",218,10,"C","m"],["t",218,164,"C","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Charge sharing between conductors",q:"Two charged spherical conductors of radius R1 and R2 are connected by a wire. Then the ratio of surface charge densities of the spheres (\u03C31/\u03C32) is",o:["R\u2081\xB2/R\u2082\xB2","R\u2081/R\u2082","R\u2082/R\u2081","\u221A(R\u2081/R\u2082)"],c:2,s:`Joined by a wire, both spheres reach the same potential: V1 = V2.
V = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R), so Q1/R1 = Q2/R2.
Put Q = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2: \u03C31\xB74\u03C0R1\xB2/R1 = \u03C32\xB74\u03C0R2\xB2/R2.
This gives \u03C31R1 = \u03C32R2.
So \u03C31/\u03C32 = R2/R1.
The smaller sphere has the larger surface charge density.`,fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Two spheres of radii R1 and R2 joined by a coiled conducting wire",d:[["c",65,80,55],["c",235,80,55],["c",65,80,1.5,"F"],["c",235,80,1.5,"F"],["l",65,80,10,80],["l",235,80,290,80],["t",28,98,"R\u2081"],["t",262,98,"R\u2082"],["l",120,80,129,80],["c",134,80,4.5],["c",143,80,4.5],["c",152,80,4.5],["c",161,80,4.5],["c",170,80,4.5],["l",175,80,180,80],["t",150,56,"Conducting","ms"],["t",150,68,"wire","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Energy stored in capacitor",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has a uniform electric field ' E ' in the space between the plates. If the distance between the plates is 'd' and the area of each plate is 'A', the energy stored in the capacitor is (\u03B50 = permittivity of free space)",o:["E\xB2Ad/\u03B5\u2080","(1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2","\u03B5\u2080EAd","(1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad"],c:3,s:`The energy is stored in the field between the plates.
Energy per unit volume of an electric field is u = (1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2.
Volume between the plates = A \xD7 d.
U = u \xD7 volume = (1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2 \xD7 Ad.`,fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"Parallel plate capacitor, area A, separation d, uniform field E upward from the plus plate to the minus plate",d:[["p","M30 55 L200 55 L260 25 L90 25 Z"],["p","M30 120 L200 120 L260 90 L90 90 Z"],["t",62,50,"\u2013 \u2013 \u2013 \u2013 \u2013","s"],["t",34,134,"+ + + + + + +","s"],["l",50,112,50,66,"a"],["l",85,112,85,66,"a"],["l",120,112,120,66,"a"],["l",155,112,155,66,"a"],["t",12,92,"E"],["l",200,56,200,119,"b"],["t",208,78,"d"],["t",150,152,"Plate area = A","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Combination of capacitors",q:"The equivalent capacitance of the combination shown in the figure is",o:["3C/2","3C","2C","C/2"],c:2,s:`The middle capacitor has its two plates joined by wires to the same two end points (marked 1, 2, 3 in the solution), so both plates are at one potential.
It is short-circuited and stores no charge.
The other two capacitors C and C are in parallel between A and B.
Ceq = C + C = 2C.`,fig:{w:260,h:110,alt:"Three equal capacitors C: one in the top branch, one in the bottom branch and one vertical in the middle joining the two wires, between two terminals",d:[["l",22,50,50,50],["l",215,50,240,50],["l",50,23,50,78],["l",215,23,215,78],["l",50,23,79,23],["l",85,23,215,23],["l",50,78,79,78],["l",85,78,215,78],["l",79,16,79,30],["l",85,16,85,30],["l",79,71,79,85],["l",85,71,85,85],["l",145,23,145,44],["l",145,56,145,78],["c",145,23,1.8,"F"],["c",145,78,1.8,"F"],["l",137,47,153,47],["l",137,53,153,53],["t",82,14,"C","m"],["t",82,100,"C","m"],["t",158,54,"C"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:245,alt:"Given circuit with points 1, 2, 3 marked, then redrawn as two capacitors C in parallel between A and B",d:[["c",20,67,3],["l",23,67,55,67],["l",220,67,255,67],["c",258,67,3],["l",55,40,55,95],["l",220,40,220,95],["l",55,40,137,40],["l",143,40,220,40],["l",55,95,137,95],["l",143,95,220,95],["l",137,33,137,47],["l",143,33,143,47],["l",137,88,137,102],["l",143,88,143,102],["t",140,31,"C","m"],["t",140,113,"C","m"],["t",20,88,"A","m"],["t",266,88,"B","m"],["l",185,40,185,62],["l",185,74,185,95],["c",185,40,2,"F"],["c",185,95,2,"F"],["c",220,67,2,"F"],["l",177,65,193,65],["l",177,71,193,71],["t",190,34,"1","s"],["t",224,82,"2","s"],["t",190,108,"3","s"],["t",198,72,"C"],["c",20,187,3],["l",23,187,55,187],["l",220,187,255,187],["c",258,187,3],["l",55,160,55,215],["l",220,160,220,215],["l",55,160,137,160],["l",143,160,220,160],["l",55,215,137,215],["l",143,215,220,215],["l",137,153,137,167],["l",143,153,143,167],["l",137,208,137,222],["l",143,208,143,222],["t",140,151,"C","m"],["t",140,233,"C","m"],["t",20,206,"A","m"],["t",280,206,"B","m"],["t",226,208,"1, 2, 3","s"],["c",220,187,2,"F"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Merging of drops",q:"Twenty seven drops of same size are charged at 200 V each. They combine to form a bigger drop. Calculate the potential of the bigger drop.",o:["1980 V","660 V","1320 V","1520 V"],c:0,s:`Volume stays the same: 27 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 3r.
Charge is conserved: Q = 27q.
V = kQ/R = k\xB727q/(3r) = 9(kq/r) = 9 \xD7 V_small.
With the potential of one drop as 200 V this gives 1800 V, which is not an option.
With 220 V (the value used in the printed solution) it gives 9 \xD7 220 = 1980 V, which is option 1.
Note: Printed answer 1 (1980 V) needs a small-drop potential of 220 V; the question prints 200 V, which would give 1800 V (not an option). Printed key kept.`}]})();
